---
import ArrowUpRight from '../components/ArrowUpRight.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { breadcrumbList } from '../lib/schema';
import { SITE_URL } from '../site';

const title = "You're Not Late. You're Still on Tools. | Danny McGiffin";
const description =
	'Most $5–50M companies already use AI. Almost none have redesigned how the core work gets done. Score one real workflow.';
const pageUrl = `${SITE_URL}/still-on-tools/`;

const structuredData = [
	breadcrumbList([
		{ name: 'Home', url: `${SITE_URL}/` },
		{ name: "You're not late. You're still on tools.", url: pageUrl }
	])
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} structuredData={structuredData}>
	<SiteHeader />
	<main id="main-content">
		<header class="offer-hero section-dark">
			<div class="shell">
				<p class="eyebrow">Research briefing for $5–50M founders and operators</p>
				<h1 class="display">You're not late.<br /><em>You're still on tools.</em></h1>
				<p class="offer-deck">
					Most mid-market companies already use AI. Almost none have changed how the core work gets done. That
					gap is still small. It will not stay small.
				</p>
				<p>
					<a class="button button--primary" href="/score-one-workflow/"
						>Score one workflow <ArrowUpRight /></a
					>
				</p>
				<p class="offer-deck">Ten short questions. One real workflow. Not a company-wide AI score.</p>
			</div>
		</header>

		<article class="prose service-prose shell">
			<p>
				If your company is using ChatGPT, Copilot, or the AI features inside software you already pay for, you
				are not behind.
			</p>
			<p>You are in the main body of the market.</p>
			<p>
				The mid-market surveys from 2025–2026 say the same thing in different words. Tools are everywhere.
				Meeting notes, document drafts, a first pass on marketing. The work is faster in spots. The process
				underneath is mostly the same.
			</p>
			<p>
				That is a legitimate place to start. The cheap path — a person opens a browser tool and gets a draft back
				— is how almost everyone entered. Kaufman Rossin’s 2026 US mid-market survey put it plainly: generative
				AI “requires no process redesign.”
			</p>
			<p>The next gap is different.</p>
			<p>
				A small group of companies is not adding another copilot. They are changing how the work actually moves:
				the SOP, the handoff, the system of record. If you took the AI out, the process would break or have to be
				redesigned. That is rare — and where a measurable gap starts to open.
			</p>
			<p>For a $5–50M company, this is still early. It will not stay that way.</p>

			<h2>What the research actually shows</h2>
			<p>
				Seven locked sources from 2025–2026 sit behind this briefing. None isolate $5–50M revenue. Closest cuts:
				Kaufman Rossin’s 20–99 employee group, McKinsey’s global firms under $100M, RSM’s $30M–$10B AI users.
				Those numbers are not averaged.
			</p>
			<p>Two levels matter.</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Assisted steps.</strong> AI helps a discrete task inside a process that is still basically the
				same. A person still runs the process. If you turned the tool off, the work would slow down. It would not
				be redesigned.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>Redesigned work.</strong> The process was reworked so AI is load-bearing. Adjacent steps changed.
				The SOP, the handoff, or the system of record changed. If you turned the AI off, you would have a
				different process.
			</p>
			<p>Most companies that “use AI” are in the first group. The second is the scarce one.</p>

			<h3>1. Tools are common. Operational maturity is not.</h3>
			<p>
				In Kaufman Rossin’s US sample of 100 mid-market decision-makers ($5M to under $1B, 20 to under 1,000
				employees), 94% have AI tools. Generative AI leads at 70%. The top uses are meeting notes (77%), data
				analysis (73%), document processing (67%), marketing (65%), and knowledge search (64%) — help bolted onto
				existing work.
			</p>
			<p>
				The same survey’s “AI iceberg” is the operational tell. Tools sit at 94%. Integration / iPaaS sits at
				33%. RPA sits at 31%. Without that connective tissue, the typical path is copy-paste: the model produces
				something useful, and a person still carries it into the system of record.
			</p>
			<p>
				Kaufman Rossin calls this the “messy middle.” 52% are Testers. 31% are Builders — AI in at least one core
				process, an upper bound on redesigned work, not a count of it. Fully embedded Operators: 2%. Separately,
				73% say their processes are still early or foundational.
			</p>
			<p>
				Larger mid-market samples look louder and land in the same place. RSM’s 1,030 AI-using firms
				($30M–$10B, non-adopters excluded) say 86% have “integrated AI into operations.” RSM’s own reading:
				without a shift in how work gets done, AI remains “a layer of productivity tooling.”
			</p>

			<h3>2. The scarce thing is redesigned work, not another assistant.</h3>
			<p>Assisted steps are not scarce. Redesigned workflows are.</p>
			<p>
				McKinsey’s 2025 global survey is the cleanest measure we have, and it is not a $5–50M sample. Among
				AI-using respondents, 20% of the non-high-performers said they had “fundamentally redesigned individual
				workflows.” Among the high-performer tail — roughly 6% of the sample, defined by more than 5% of EBIT
				from AI plus “significant value” — that figure was 55%. Workflow redesign was among the strongest
				correlates of impact of the 31 factors tested.
			</p>
			<p>
				Even in Deloitte’s 2026 “leading-edge” global sample of companies that already have AI in daily use, 37%
				report little or no change to existing processes. 30% say they are redesigning key processes.
				Larger-firm, often AI-user-only numbers — and already a minority. True redesigned work in $5–50M
				companies is not measured here. Expect it well below those rates.
			</p>
			<p>
				Agentic AI does not change the picture. McKinsey found 62% experimenting with agents and 23% scaling an
				agentic system somewhere — no more than 10% in any one function. An agent on an unchanged process is
				still an assisted step.
			</p>

			<h3>3. The smaller-firm evidence is thin. It still points earlier.</h3>
			<p>
				Kaufman Rossin’s 20–99 employee cut is the closest small-firm signal in this set. It is not a $5–50M
				revenue cut. It sits inside a 100-person US sample that runs from $5M to under $1B, so the cell is small
				and directional only.
			</p>
			<p>
				Inside that sample, 60% of 20–99 employee firms had reached Tester or Builder, versus 83% overall, 88%
				at 100–499 employees, and 96% at 500–999. More of the smallest firms were still in unofficial, individual
				use.
			</p>
			<p>
				McKinsey’s global size cut — a different sample, and a looser test — points the same way. 29% of firms
				under $100M had reached the “scaling” phase, versus nearly half of firms above $5 billion. Scaling there
				means breadth of deployment, not redesigned work.
			</p>
			<p>
				Do not read those as a $5–50M prevalence rate. Do read the direction: the smaller cuts we have look
				earlier than an already-messy mid-market average. That is mostly capacity — IT benches, data platforms,
				slack — not a verdict on intent. The useful implication is the reverse of “you are behind.” If companies
				at your scale are still mostly on tools, the ones who redesign one load-bearing workflow now are doing it
				against a field that has not moved yet.
			</p>

			<h3>4. The companies pulling ahead changed the process, not the tool count.</h3>
			<p>
				No source runs a causal study of $5–50M firms. The same differentiators show up anyway.
			</p>
			<p>
				They change the next step in the chain. McKinsey’s manifesto test is the cleanest version: a model that
				predicts equipment failures does nothing if maintenance still follows the calendar. The prediction is an
				assisted step. Changing how work is scheduled is redesigned work. McKinsey’s high performers are 2.8×
				more likely to have fundamentally redesigned workflows (55% vs 20%).
			</p>
			<p>
				They pick one or two load-bearing workflows instead of a long use-case list. They build the stack that
				lets an AI output <em>act</em> — data, integration, a rule for when a person has to touch the work.
				Human-in-the-loop validation: 65% of McKinsey high performers vs 23% of others.
			</p>
			<p>
				They measure whether the business moved, not whether the pilot felt good. RSM’s AI users report 97%
				satisfaction, mostly on time and efficiency. Only 44% of Kaufman Rossin’s sample track financial ROI,
				even though 95% agree AI saves time. Time saved is real. It is not a redesigned process.
			</p>
			<p>
				The barriers are not mysterious. Kaufman Rossin: cybersecurity (43%), talent (42%), legacy integration
				(41%). Executive sponsorship is last, at 12%. Mid-market firms are not waiting for the CEO to care. They
				are stuck on the foundations that let a tool become a process.
			</p>

			<h2>Why the next year matters</h2>
			<p>
				Do not import the headlines. RSM’s 36% “fully embedded,” Netrio’s 26% “scaled and governed,” and
				Deloitte’s 30% “redesigning key processes” come from larger, often AI-user-only samples. They are not
				your base rate. The installed base is already copilots and document help. Another tool of that type is
				how you stay in the messy middle with better software.
			</p>
			<p>
				The urgency is not that you missed AI. You did not. The urgency is that spend and expectations are
				already moving, and most of that motion is still buying help for unchanged work.
			</p>
			<p>
				Kaufman Rossin: 93% plan to increase AI spending in the next 12 months. RSM: 84% expect spend to rise
				next year. Among RSM respondents increasing that spend, 41% are cutting cybersecurity and 40% are
				cutting external consulting. That is how a company can feel busy on AI and be in the same place a year
				from now: more tools, same process, a slightly faster messy middle.
			</p>
			<p>
				The companies that will look different in twelve months are not the ones with the most licenses. They
				are the ones who pick one workflow that binds the business and change the adjacent steps. That work takes
				months, not a Friday afternoon. Starting it while most peers are still on unofficial tools is the open
				window. Waiting for a cleaner average is how you arrive after the people who changed the process have
				already begun to pull away.
			</p>
			<p>
				You do not need to become an AI-native company this year. Almost nobody is. You do need to know, for one
				real workflow, whether you are still on tools.
			</p>

			<h2>Score one workflow</h2>
			<p>
				Take one workflow you actually care about — invoice-to-cash, onboarding, quote-to-fulfillment, the weekly
				close — and run the free assessment. Ten short questions. Same process-change test as this research. A
				plain label: Shadow, Assisted, or Redesigned.
			</p>
			<p>
				If that workflow binds cash, delivery, or a customer, the paid diagnostic is a $5,000 Workflow Teardown.
				It maps and costs the real work, redesigns the workflow, and defines what should be built. The full fee is
				credited toward an approved Fixed-Price Workflow Build.
			</p>
			<p>
				<a href="/score-one-workflow/">Score one workflow →</a>
			</p>

			<h2>A reading, not a deck</h2>
			<p>
				The seams are usually where the money is, and they are usually already written down — in two files
				nobody ever puts on the same page. How to do that join is
				<a href="/writing/your-vendor-already-told-you-what-it-doesnt-do/">here</a>. It is not a strategy deck.
			</p>

			<h2>From score to build</h2>
			<p><strong>Score one workflow</strong> — Free. A label for one workflow.</p>
			<p><strong>Workflow Teardown</strong> — $5,000. A two-week operational diagnostic.</p>
			<p>
				If the workflow is worth redesigning and we agree on the implementation, the next step is a Fixed-Price
				Workflow Build.
			</p>
			<p>
				If there is nothing worth building, the Teardown says so. You keep the findings and are not pushed into
				unnecessary implementation work.
			</p>
		</article>
	</main>
	<SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
